{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the Kamo River like confetti. No guidebook could’ve prepared me for the quiet magic of Gion’s wooden machiya houses, or the scent of matcha wafting from a hidden teahouse where an elderly woman bowed as she poured my tea with hands that remembered centuries.\n\nI rode the bullet train to Hiroshima, where the Peace Memorial Park stood in solemn silence. A single origami crane, left by a child, sat folded on the memorial’s edge. I bought one too—and wrote a wish on it: “May we remember, and choose kindness.”\n\nTokyo was a symphony of neon and stillness. In Shibuya, crowds surged like ocean tides around the crossing, yet no one rushed—everyone moved with purpose, polite, unhurried. I ate ramen at 2 a.m. in a tiny alleyway stall, where the chef nodded without a word and handed me a bowl so rich it felt like warmth made edible.\n\nIn Nikko, I wandered past torii gates draped in moss, their red fading gently into the forest. The air smelled of cedar and rain. A monk smiled as I passed, offering a small stone inscribed with “和” — harmony. I kept it in my pocket.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers: in the rustle of a kimono, the clink of a tea cup, the bow of a stranger on the train. It teaches you to slow down, to notice the space between breaths, to find awe in the ordinary.\n\nI didn’t buy much. Just a wooden comb, a tin of hojicha, and the quiet certainty that some places don’t just change your itinerary—they change your soul.\n\nI left with more than souvenirs. I left with stillness.\n\n— 297 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 